  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q:pains-first-call",
      "question": "On the first call with your last ten customers, which pain did they name before you named anything - and which one did the churned accounts name on the way out?",
      "why": "This map is built entirely from public strangers. Not one source in it is a customer or a prospect of yours, so every row is a hypothesis about what your buyers would say, ranked by how loudly other people said it. Your first-call notes would re-rank the whole list and tell me which of these eight are actually the ones that close.",
      "would_change": "the order of pains[], pains[].confidence on every row, and which rows survive into the ICP step"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-offline-moment",
      "question": "When a customer tells you offline is a dealbreaker, what is the story they tell - what were they trying to do, where were they, and what happened?",
      "why": "pains[].shape.personal on pain:offline-blackout is null. Every public source stops at the missing capability or the switch away; nobody says what it cost them. The personal rung is the one that sells, and I will not invent it.",
      "would_change": "pains[].shape.personal and pains[].shape.personal_note on pain:offline-blackout"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-stale-page-cost",
      "question": "Has a customer ever told you about something that went wrong because a page in their workspace was out of date - a decision made on it, a process followed from it?",
      "why": "pains[].shape.personal on pain:no-way-to-know-if-its-still-true is null for the same reason. People describe the workaround and the wish but never the consequence, and the consequence is the story.",
      "would_change": "pains[].shape.personal and pains[].shape.personal_note on pain:no-way-to-know-if-its-still-true"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-freshness-awareness",
      "question": "Of customers using the knowledge base, what share know the page verification and recheck-cycle feature exists - and do any of them ask for it as if it were missing?",
      "why": "The strongest finding in this map is that a practitioner wished out loud for a freshness stamp and said he was sure it existed somewhere, and another practitioner replied that Notion already ships it. If your own customers do the same, this is a latent pain and a messaging gap, which is a cheap fix. If they all know about it, the row should be felt instead and ranked lower.",
      "would_change": "pains[].type and pains[].revealed_by on pain:no-way-to-know-if-its-still-true"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-seat-charge-volume",
      "question": "How many support tickets a month are people saying they were charged for seats they did not mean to add, and what share end in a refund?",
      "why": "The two quotes behind pain:accidental-seat-charge are near identical and from two strangers, but both were read on Trustpilot, which only collects people angry enough to post. I cannot tell from the public record whether this is two loud people or a systematic leak. Your ticket volume settles it in one number.",
      "would_change": "pains[].confidence on pain:accidental-seat-charge, and whether it stays in the map at all"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-ai-first-tradeoff",
      "question": "Since the site and signup flow went AI-first, what happened to activation for people who came for docs and wiki - do they reach a first written page faster or slower than before?",
      "why": "pain:ai-first-buries-the-basics is the uncomfortable one: four buyers describe hunting for the core product behind the AI. That is either a real cost you are paying knowingly for a bigger prize, or an accident. Your activation numbers are the only thing that can tell the difference, and the angles step needs to know before it writes a hook.",
      "would_change": "pains[].confidence on pain:ai-first-buries-the-basics, and awareness.default"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-meeting-and-calendar",
      "question": "What did customers say to you about AI meeting notes and Notion Calendar - what were they doing before, and what did they stop doing after?",
      "why": "This is the feature-in-search-of-a-problem check, and it came back empty. feat:meeting and feat:calendar have no pain pointing at them in this map, because across every thread and review read, no buyer complained about a problem either one solves. That silence is a finding the positioning and offer steps need, but it is only honest if I say where I looked: Hacker News and Trustpilot, with Reddit and G2 unreachable in this run.",
      "would_change": "a new row in pains[] and pains[].feature_ref on it"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-export-contradiction",
      "question": "When someone leaves, how long does the export actually take them and what breaks in it?",
      "why": "The public record contradicts itself. Several people describe Notion as lock-in like Sharepoint or Confluence, while ahmadyan, who actually left, thanks the team for making export so easy and says the second migration was much easier than the first. I did not make lock-in a pain because of that contradiction. Your own offboarding data resolves whether there is a pain here or only a fear.",
      "would_change": "whether a lock-in row enters pains[], and pains[].shape.operational on pain:offline-blackout"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-prosumer-drift",
      "question": "Do your individual and solopreneur customers say the product is being built for someone else now, and does it show up in their renewals?",
      "why": "Three people in one thread say versions of this - features walled behind higher tiers, updates that serve corporate needs, a product that no longer cares about personal wikis. Three voices from one thread is not a pattern, so it is not a row here. If your prosumer churn agrees with them, it is a pain and seg:individual needs its own row.",
      "would_change": "a new row in pains[] scoped to seg:individual, and awareness for seg:individual"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-editor-friction",
      "question": "When customers write something long in Notion - a design doc, a post, a spec - what do they complain about, and do any of them draft it somewhere else first?",
      "why": "Three separate people describe fighting the editor itself: long-form writing being arduous, text that will not select without the keyboard, tables that make one of them want to throw a laptop in the ocean. I left it out because the other eight rows have deeper evidence, but if your customers draft elsewhere and paste in, that is a pain with a workaround and belongs in the map.",
      "would_change": "a new row in pains[] with feature_ref feat:docs"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-enterprise-voice",
      "question": "Can you put me in front of two or three IT or ops people who standardised a workspace on Notion, or point me at call recordings with them?",
      "why": "seg:enterprise is the thinnest segment in this map and it is not for lack of sweeping. Economic buyers at that size do not post in public: pain:docs-here-tickets-there ended up with no economic_buyer role at all because every voice I found was an internal advocate rather than a budget owner, and the enterprise rows elsewhere lean on two practitioners who happened to mention their tenant and their clients. Every enterprise claim in this artifact is weaker than it looks and I would rather say so than pad it.",
      "would_change": "pains[].who_feels and pains[].by_role.economic_buyer on pain:docs-here-tickets-there, pains[].segments, and awareness for seg:enterprise"
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "src:1",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41683860",
      "title": "Comment by base698 on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I just migrated everything out of Notion into Ibsidian.  Notion was unusable after more than a few hundred items in a DB.  I migrated to using dataview and lists in Obsidian and haven't had a problem since.  And it works offline.\n\nI use it for my workout logbook and cooking/recipe log with hundreds of entries."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:2",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47761945",
      "title": "Comment by pcchristie on \"Introduction to Obsidian\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2026-04-14",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I migrated from Notion to Obsidian, mainly because of speed, and to a lesser extent I like the philosophy.\n\nHaving simultaneously embraced AI a lot more, it's been incredible having AI very easily access and work with my notes for me (not to mention making the migration so much better by brute forcing a lot of the tidying and database formatting).\n\nThe only thing I haven't been able to replicate from Notion is my shopping list."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:3",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=37278531",
      "title": "Comment by lucw on \"Show HN: Open-source obsidian.md sync server\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. The commenter is a paying Obsidian Sync user comparing it against Notion, so read as a buyer of the category rather than a neutral party",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2023-08-27",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Super happy Obsidian Sync user here. I migrated over from Notion, here are the areas where Obsidian blows Notion out of the water:\n\n- Insanely low input lag especially on mobile. Obsidian is FAST. When i'm out and about checking my shopping list, I have a tolerance for maybe 250ms of lag. That's it. You need to show me my data FAST. Notion is a basically a webapp which simply doesn't compete, asks you to re-login constantly. YOU DO NOT ASK A MOBILE USER to RELOGIN.\n\n- Read-only mode: accidental taps instead of scrolls on mobile are a HUGE issue on Notion, especially when you're out and you're in a stressful situation and you need your notes fast."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:4",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41772779",
      "title": "Comment by LizPoggi on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Commenter identifies elsewhere in the same thread as a solopreneur using Notion daily",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2024-10-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Agreed. Not useful for large dataset. The magic is in connecting databases - relating project management to notes etc. I've resorted to duplicating each database and archiving anything that is worth keeping. That way databases I use operationally retain speed."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:5",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41688838",
      "title": "Comment by remram on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I have the opposite complaint: you can't even reliably select text without also using the keyboard! Half the time I drag to select, it starts moving a block. Half the time I click to navigate, it starts a selection instead (as if I had not released the mouse from previous click). I need to press the Escape key and try again.\n\nNotion to me is the prime example for \"too much JavaScript\". Opening a note in a tab take 10 seconds and interrupts me half the time with some random news or a prompt to try some AI feature."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:6",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41706017",
      "title": "Comment by ksec on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Commenter is speaking for a team that uses Notion, so read as the internal champion",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2024-10-01",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I am reading all the comments and I am feel very different with the general sentiments here.\n\nNotion is like Excel. It isn't or wasn't meant to be used in certain ways, but its simplicity and flexility meant 99% of the world is running on Excel. From SMEs to Fortune 500s. Because that is what normal non-tech users, from managers to exec know how to use and understand.\n\nThere are a lot I dont like about Notion. Mostly on performance. But I valued it a lot because that is how 70% of my team understand and can continue to work together."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:7",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41622351",
      "title": "Comment by achristmascarl on \"Linear Mobile – Available for iOS and Android\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2024-09-23",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "this may sound strange, but i've decided to try and use Linear as a personal writing tool.\n\ni used to use Notion, but the lack of offline mode, even after all these years, made me decide to look for alternatives.\n\ni thought about obsidian, but there are wasn't a simple, free way to sync between desktop, ios, and android all at once; Google Docs also seemed like a possibility, but it doesn't seem to have markdown support on mobile (it does in the web)\n\nso, since Linear has a comparable free tier to Notion, great offline, and apps on all platforms.... here goes nothing"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:8",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=33626843",
      "title": "Comment by IncRnd on \"Notion AI – waiting list signup\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2022-11-16",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "> - Offline mode. My data is mine, my tables, templates, notes, have no reason to live in your server.\n\nI couldn't agree more than I already do.\n\nWithout this \"feature\" I will never use Notion. Offline mode is a minimum for me to even look at such an information handling product."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:9",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=43383364",
      "title": "Comment by j45 on \"Docs – Open source alternative to Notion or Outline\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-03-16",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Notion is good, maybe great for some but small things holding it back from becoming ubiquitous.\n\nDoes notion work offline-first yet?\n\nThe only copy of my data should not exist solely in an app's cloud, and I should not need to manually export anything.\n\nCollaboration is nice, still has nothing to do with being offline-first."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:10",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=39030569",
      "title": "Comment by PurpleRamen on \"Notion Calendar\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2024-01-17",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Still no offline-mode? Notion is a really great tool, but I really want my data local to tinker with them in all kind of ways which Notion was not made to support. And all the clones and alternatives are still years away from Notion's level of polishing. Really strange."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:11",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41372905",
      "title": "Comment by nunez on \"Just use fucking paper, man\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-08-27",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I don't understand why Notion is so damn popular. It's a textbook example of \"jack of all trades; master of none.\"\n\nIt has databases that allow you to create many different views of things, but actually using this capability is one of the most frustrating things ever. No keyboard shortcuts; actions that you'd typically do in Excel don't work here; every table is its own page, even when it doesn't need to be; and so on.\n\nIf you try to use Notion off-line, it randomly won't show you changes until you're back online.\n\nI will say that trying to modify a table in Notion makes me want to throw my Mac into the ocean and go back to paper and briefcases."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:12",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41687113",
      "title": "Comment by redsparrow on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "> a collection of half-finished documents that are always out of date\n\nI think of company wikis as a place where information goes to die.\n\nA useful feature, which I'm sure exists somewhere, would be \"freshness\" checks on pages. A timestamp for the last time someone looked at this and said \"yes, this is still valid\". For pages that are important, a team could set up recurring tasks for people to do periodic freshness checks.\n\nSurely this is already a common practice, although not any team I've been on. Undoubtedly there is some ISO-9000 process for this..."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:13",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40000081",
      "title": "Comment by koliber on \"Ask HN: Best tools for creating user and developer documentation for web apps?\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-04-11",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "For internal documentation, I go for a mix of:\n\n- in-code comments with links to more in-depth resources\n\n- a documentation directory in some projects and modules that contains markdown\n\n- a Notion internal wiki maintained by all the developers\n\nMaking documentation easy to navigate, clean to read, and able to communicate depends more on the people writing the documentation than the tool. A plain text file can be great or horrible in those regards. That applies to all other documentation mechanisms as well.\n\nCompanies often don't suffer from a lack of documentation, but from poorly organized, maintained, and curated documentation. The problem is finding what you are looking for. For looking up documentation, the two broad approaches are search and clicking-through-links. Search is broken, and compartmentalized to tools."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:14",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=33625639",
      "title": "Comment by koliber on \"Notion AI – waiting list signup\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Same commenter as src:13 eighteen months earlier, so the two are one person's voice across two threads, not two strangers",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2022-11-16",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I am a raving Notion fan. It's one of the few products that I just adore.\n\nMaybe my tolerance level is high, or maybe my hardware is good, but actual performance is acceptable.\n\nI do hope that Notion does improve offline mode. A single page works that is already loaded works wonderfully. Syncing has been non-problematic for me. I wonder why my phone and mac can't cache all the pages indefinitely, which would allow me to work completely offline.\n\nAlso, search needs work. Both search from the \"search button\" and the search that pops up when I type \"@bla\" are becoming less useful with each additional page in my workspace."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:15",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=33625746",
      "title": "Comment by Hamuko on \"Notion AI – waiting list signup\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2022-11-16",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": ">Particular in the corporate space, the problem of finding relevant knowledge\n\nI'm eagerly waiting for the day that I don't get frustrated with Notion's search. I'm not sure if I actually even bother with the search that much these days. I just try to drill down the page structure to find what looks relevant."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:16",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=44815462",
      "title": "Comment by hnlmorg on \"Google suffers data breach in ongoing Salesforce data theft attacks\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Commenter describes working at and consulting for several companies, so the voice is a practitioner reporting across clients",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-08-06",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "You also have to bear in mind that organising docs is an endless and thankless job which nobody wants to do. So these things tend to multiply like vermin once someone starts creating docs on another platform. One startup I worked for somehow managed to have stuff scattered between Confluence, Notion and Google Docs despite only employing 50 people. It was crazy.\n\nAnother client I recently worked for had Sharepoint, Notion and Confluence as their official tools for documentation.\n\nAt this point in time, it's easier to just accept that each org is going to end up with multiple overlapping solutions because you'll get new people join the team and they'll want to use their preferred tool because that's what they're productive in and so the spiral continues."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:17",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41687956",
      "title": "Comment by more_corn on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Try writing documentation in confluence and you'll get why Notion is preferable. Also go try to find something you wrote in confluence six months ago."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:18",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=43694965",
      "title": "Comment by alexey-salmin on \"Notion Mail is out\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2025-04-15",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Surely this could be addressed by putting more AI on top.\n\nOn a serious note, my team is very happy we migrated from Notion to Linear for task tracking last year, but we're still looking how to cover the wiki part."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:19",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=28457757",
      "title": "Comment by sanedigital on \"Notion Acquires Automate.io\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Commenter speaks for a company that changed tools, but nothing in the comment shows he owned the budget, which is why he is read as a champion and not an economic buyer",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2021-09-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "After fighting the move to Jira/Confluence for years, my company finally migrated away from Notion & Clubhouse. I couldn't be happier. Notion was great when we were <5 people, but it feels like a toy compared to Confluence."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:20",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41689326",
      "title": "Comment by vunderba on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Counterpoint, I have seen sync issues on very popular large note, cloud-based solutions that use proprietary formats - Evernote comes to mind.\n\nWhen you have tens of thousands of notes, it's hard to even know if at some point, the note has been suddenly changed, reverted, or modified in a way that you didn't even realize occurred.\n\nWith an open format, specifically a text based one like Markdown, I can sync all my notes to a git repository in a diffable manner that I can quickly review."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:21",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41685532",
      "title": "Comment by light_hue_1 on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Commenter self-describes as an ML researcher who uses LLMs daily, which is why the objection reads as a governance position rather than an anti-AI one",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "That isn't reassuring at all. It's pretty much the worst case scenario. I don't want my private notes sent to places like openai who will use them to train models and who don't have any reasonable corporate governance. They have leaked customer data before.\n\nAnd I'm not anti AI. I'm an ML researcher who uses LLMs all the time. But there's a big difference between that and revealing all my private information to them."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:22",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41687586",
      "title": "Comment by nunez on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. Commenter is speaking as someone who administers a Notion tenant, which is the only enterprise-admin voice found in this sweep",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "You can turn Notion AI off in your tenant. It takes a few days, but support will do it. It's annoying that we have to ask instead of toggling a switch, but there you go."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:23",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46538702",
      "title": "Comment by hxugufjfjf on \"Notion AI: Unpatched data exfiltration\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-01-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "As much as I love using Notion, they have a terrible track record when it comes to dealing with and responding to security issues."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:24",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46535350",
      "title": "Comment by someguyiguess on \"Notion AI: Unpatched data exfiltration\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-01-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Wow what a coincidence. I just migrated from notion to obsidian today. Looks like I timed it perfectly (or maybe slightly too late?)"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:25",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45527240",
      "title": "Comment by jmuguy on \"McKinsey wonders how to sell AI apps with no measurable benefits\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2025-10-09",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I hadn't ever tried Notion before but I sort of vaguely understood it was a nice way to make some documentation and wiki type content.  I had a need for something like a table that I could filter that I would normally just do in Google Sheets.  So I go check out Notion and their entire site is focused on AI.  Look at what this agent can do, or that.  I signed up and the entire signup flow is also focused on AI.  Finally I was able to locate what I thought was their core offering - the wikis etc.  And ended up pretty impressed with the features they have for all of that.\n\nNow maybe Notion customers love all these AI features but it was super weird to see that stuff so prominently given my understanding of what the company was all about."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:26",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=27169183",
      "title": "Comment by auslegung on \"Notion has been inaccessible for nearly 24 hours\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2021-05-15",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "This is why I'm concerned about companies putting all of their documentation in Notion without an offline backup. But thankfully Notion just made their API public beta so it's now possible to backup everything somewhere else. I'd much prefer to use a git forge's wiki for a company's documentation."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:27",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=37743816",
      "title": "Comment by dunham on \"Notion experiencing issues (over 6 hours)\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. A former user describing what he found when he inspected Notion's sync protocol, so it is a technical account rather than a complaint",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2023-10-02",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "But the biggest impediments to offline for Notion (again when I last checked) were that it doesn't have a full copy of the data and some of the operations are executed server-side (autocomplete dropdowns, search, and some bulk operations).  I was hoping they would deal with the former, because it'd make it easier to backup and process my data.  But I ended up moving on.\n\nI think they've dealt with some of the search operations, but are still not syncing all of the blocks."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:28",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41702976",
      "title": "Comment by Fire-Dragon-DoL on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2024-09-30",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I find incredibly disappointing that so many new features have been walled behind higher tiers.\n\nNotion forgot that we pay monthly price so that they can keep developing their software to our benefit too",
      "unused": "One of three voices on the product being built for someone else now. All three are from the same thread, which is one conversation rather than a pattern, so it is not a row. Named in q:pains-prosumer-drift and kept here so the next re-run knows where to look."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:29",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41687488",
      "title": "Comment by jprete on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I really wasn't sure!\n\nBut to give you a more substantive response, I think the tastemakers are not in control in the slightest. Everything about Notion now screams \"we don't give a crap about note-taking or personal wikis, we want a piece of that juicy enterprise market\".",
      "unused": "Second of the three prosumer-drift voices, same thread as src:28. Named in q:pains-prosumer-drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:30",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41772736",
      "title": "Comment by LizPoggi on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2024-10-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Notion databases easily allow dependencies, integrated relationship tracking and multiple ways to visualize a project roadmap. Can't speak to JIRA but Notion is continuously releasing new updates that respond to user needs. There is a major downside to this of course. As a solopreneur I find it frustrating that Notion's updates tend to serve corporate needs, but it's a good business strategy and allows Notion to continue to serve others with its free version.",
      "unused": "Third prosumer-drift voice and the only one who names her own segment, but still the same thread as src:28 and src:29. Named in q:pains-prosumer-drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:31",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30393402",
      "title": "Comment by statico on \"How we use Notion as a startup\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2022-02-19",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "This is a great list of reasons to use Notion as your company's information hub. It's basically a wiki, but with some great templating and database and access control and usability features that make it especially wonderful for teams.\n\nThe one thing holding me back from declaring that all new companies should use Notion for everything is the text editing. Doing any kind of long-form content, such as blog posts or design docs or technical documentation, is arduous and painful. The editor is usable but clunky. Editing links, selecting text, and using formatting shortcuts is wonky. Joining paragraphs is hard. If they can make editing more fluid, it might just be the perfect information organization tool.",
      "unused": "The clearest of three editor-friction voices, with src:5 and src:11 as the other two. Left out because the eight rows in the map have deeper evidence and the three describe slightly different frictions. Named in q:pains-editor-friction."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:32",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47759908",
      "title": "Comment by ahmadyan on \"Introduction to Obsidian\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum, no vendor ownership or sponsorship",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-04-14",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "A few months ago, i took the bullet and migrated my notes from Notion to .md files. This was the second time i migrated my notes (last time from Evernote to Notion), and this time it was a lot easier. Kudos to the Notion team for making export so easy.\n\nJust downloaded the notes, then told Claude to organize and remove duplicate or index mds (Notion keeps a lot of random indexes) and clean up and within 30mins i had a very clean and usable (and agent accessible) md vault.\n\nI do miss sharing capabilities as well as multi-user in Notion, so i don't think this is useful for 2player/team/corp.",
      "unused": "Counter-evidence, kept on purpose. Several people in this sweep describe Notion as lock-in; this is the one person who actually left, and he thanks the team for making export easy. That contradiction is why no lock-in pain is in pains[]. Named in q:pains-export-contradiction."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:33",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41692746",
      "title": "Comment by Majestic121 on \"Notion's mid-life crisis\"",
      "venue": "Hacker News comment thread; independent forum. This is the direct reply to redsparrow in src:12, which is what makes the pair evidence that the pain is latent",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-30",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "It's a base feature of notion, exactly as you describe it : a freshness stamp and notifications to recheck every 7/30/90/custom days"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:34",
      "url": "https://www.trustpilot.com/reviews/6a26a9dd8b9c73c6b92bfde7",
      "title": "Trustpilot review of Notion by Chloe Lin: \"Extremely misleading UI and cold customer support—charged $830+ for an accidental click!\"",
      "venue": "Trustpilot, one star; independent review site, but a venue that collects people angry enough to post, which is why the pain it supports is scored medium and not high",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-06",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I am deeply shocked and disappointed by Notion's highly misleading UI/UX layout and their inflexible customer service. While simply trying to share content from my workspace, the platform's interface implicitly added users as \"Members\" instead of \"Guests\" and automatically upgraded my workspace to a high-tier Business Yearly plan. I had absolutely no intention of upgrading, nor did I realize a massive charge was being processed. I only discovered this huge financial burden weeks later on my credit card statement. Upon realizing the mistake, I immediately went into my settings and deleted all additional members to correct the workspace. I am a single, individual user who has absolutely no need for enterprise-level features. I reached out to Notion support explaining the situation, but the agent (Maurice) completely hid behind their rigid \"annual refund policy window,\" refusing to issue even a prorated refund for the remaining 11 months of a service I am not using and have already canceled in my settings. Forcing an individual to pay over $830 USD for an accidental click during a content-sharing process is completely unfair and predatory."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:35",
      "url": "https://www.trustpilot.com/reviews/6a01e2d3fbb831c7111cb5aa",
      "title": "Trustpilot review of Notion by Amber: \"Accidental member charges with no warning — and no recourse\"",
      "venue": "Trustpilot, one star; independent review site, same complaint-skewed venue as src:34, which is the limitation on this pain",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-05",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I shared a document with family members on Notion and accidentally added them as Members instead of Guests. There was no friction, no confirmation step, nothing that hinted this would result in an annual paid seat. I only found out when I was charged over $100.\n\nWhen I contacted support, I was just outside their 3-day refund window — which was enough to shut the conversation down completely. I wasn't even asking for a full refund, just a prorated credit for months I'll never use. They refused.\n\nI'm not surprised to find that many other users have fallen into the exact same trap. At some point, a pattern this consistent stops being an accident."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:36",
      "url": "https://www.trustpilot.com/reviews/6aa306ce3b8a636e2d1861f5",
      "title": "Trustpilot review of Notion by Travis Potter: \"Notion Sends Your Proprietary Business Data to OpenAI\"",
      "venue": "Trustpilot, one star; independent review site. Reviewer writes as a business owner, so read as the person who would block a rollout",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-09",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Turns out notion was sending all of our proprietary business data to OpenAI. If you read the small print you can find this, but it should BE FRONT AND CENTER. As a business it is extremely alarming that this would be in a hard to find place especially given the fact that OpenAI has been training on data lately submitted by mathematicians to scoop them. I would be wary of trusting Notion with your sensitive data. They do not seem to be default on the side of their customers."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:37",
      "url": "https://www.trustpilot.com/reviews/6a2d4d8269dbd50c2e8c8c5b",
      "title": "Trustpilot review of Notion by Calapso: \"What a load of c**p\"",
      "venue": "Trustpilot, one star; independent review site, complaint-skewed venue",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-06",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "What a load of c**p! Just installed on my android and it went straight to AI. No visible menus nor anyway to navigate to get started. If the is then it is well hidden! During installation it prompted me to install the Notion Calendar. I did and it just sits there when you open it and does nothing. No user friendly guidance to get you started. Unless I am missing something, don't bother."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:38",
      "url": "https://www.trustpilot.com/reviews/6a27224c659d6c49e0e14412",
      "title": "Trustpilot review of Notion by Silvareno: \"From Blessing to Financial Nightmare -Warning: Token Extortion\"",
      "venue": "Trustpilot, one star; independent review site, complaint-skewed venue. Used only for the allowance stat, not for a pain row",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-06",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "1. The Unaffordable Token System: Notion has created a system that relies on agents being constantly active in the background. That is the only way they can reliably remind you of appointments, create briefings, and sync information in real-time. But this is exactly where the trap snaps shut: every move, every reminder, every single action by these agents costs tokens. What starts as a seemingly fair price turns into a bottomless pit within a very short time. For a system that must work 24/7 to fulfill its purpose, charging per token is unethical and financially non-viable."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:39",
      "url": "https://www.trustpilot.com/reviews/6a2fdd1c2fc65faddba45304",
      "title": "Trustpilot review of Notion by Ivor Pocrnja: \"No real customer support at all\"",
